<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juba II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 48 BCE–<a href="/years/23">23 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Juba II of Mauretania</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juba II was the son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-i">Juba I of Numidia</a>, raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> after his father's defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>. Augustus made him client king of Mauretania in 25 BCE, and he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-selene-ii">Cleopatra Selene</a>, daughter of Cleopatra and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a>. A learned writer, he ruled from Caesarea (modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherchell">Cherchell</a>), which later became the capital of a Roman province.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juba-ii.jpg" alt="Juba II" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marie-Lan Nguyen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juba_II_of_Mauretania_Ny_Carlsberg_Glyptotek_IN1591.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Juba II was an Amazigh prince of Numidia, born at Hippone (modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annaba">Annaba</a>), the only child and heir of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-i">Juba I</a>. His mother is unknown, though he claimed descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>. His father had been an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>, and in 46 BCE Julius Caesar defeated him at Thapsus. The infant Juba was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and walked in Caesar's triumphal procession. His biographer Duane Roller argues that he was born in early 48 BCE, though the usual estimates run from 52 to 50 BCE.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> he learned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Greek, became a Roman citizen and is said to have become one of the best educated men there. He was raised by Caesar and later by Octavian, the future Augustus, whom he accompanied on campaigns and beside whom he fought at Actium in 31 BCE. By the age of 20 he had written Roman Archaeology.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 30 BCE Octavian restored Juba as king of Numidia. About 25 BCE Augustus gave him Mauretania instead, enlarged with western Numidia, perhaps as a reward for service in a campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, while eastern Numidia was annexed to the province of Africa Proconsularis. His marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-selene-ii">Cleopatra Selene II</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii">Cleopatra VII</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a>, probably took place in 25 BCE. They made their capital at Caesarea, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherchell">Cherchell</a>, in honour of Augustus. Building at Caesarea and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volubilis">Volubilis</a> mixed Egyptian, Greek and Roman styles.</p><p>Mauretania traded across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, particularly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, exporting fish, grapes, pearls, figs, grain, furniture and purple dye from shellfish. Juba sent a party to the Purple Islands to revive the old Phoenician dye industry, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tingis</a> (modern Tangier) became a major trading centre. He supported the performing arts, the sciences and natural history. In 21 CE he made his son Ptolemy co-ruler and he died in 23 CE, to be buried with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-selene-ii">Cleopatra Selene</a> in the Royal Mausoleum of Mauretania.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Juba wrote in Greek on history, natural history, geography, grammar, painting and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>. His works include Resemblances, a comparison of Greek and Roman institutions, Libyka, On Arabia, and a Theatrical History in at least seventeen books. Only fragments survive, known from about a hundred quotations, around half in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a>'s Natural History. He collected a large library, and a monument to his writings stood in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">Gymnasium</a> of Ptolemy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. Plutarch described him as one of the most gifted rulers of his time.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Juba II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the Battle of Thapsus in 46 BCE Caesar defeated them, and <b>Juba</b>, refused entry to his own town of Zama, died in a suicide pact with the Roman general Marcus Petreius.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 6 CE they rebelled against <b>Juba II</b> and were defeated by Cossus Cornelius Lentulus, who took the title Gaetulicus.</blockquote><a href="/state/gaetuli">Gaetuli</a></li><li><blockquote>After Bocchus II bequeathed the kingdom to Rome, Augustus installed <b>Juba II</b> in 25 BCE, and Juba married Cleopatra Selene, daughter of Cleopatra VII.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-mauretania">Kingdom of Mauretania</a></li><li><blockquote>After the fall of Carthage Lixus came under the Kingdom of Mauretania, minting coins with Punic legends, and was fully absorbed under <b>Juba II</b> around 25 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/lixus">Lixus</a></li><li><blockquote>Its capital was Caesarea (Cherchell), the former royal city of <b>Juba II</b>, and it was governed by an equestrian procurator with auxiliary troops.</blockquote><a href="/state/mauretania-caesariensis">Mauretania Caesariensis</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippo-regius">Hippo Regius</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>. Died in Tipasa in Mauretania, in what is now <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.88,7.75&amp;year=-2" aria-label="Hippo Regius on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Juba II"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5529 4395h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5529 4395h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.88,7.75&amp;year=-2">What was here in 2 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
